        ":question/id": "d6e05e4c-c6f0-521f-b67c-d81eff855827",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-20853",
        ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2(t+1),\\,y=4{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${x}^{′}(0)=2\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0,$ the tangent to the curve is horizontal."
      },
