        ":question/id": "14df7ed3-c243-590c-b10b-e1904947b459",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-21072",
        ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =3\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\pi .$"
      },
